          .standout
            %p This is a sub-entry of
            %ul
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A+Survey+of+Elliptic+Cohomology"} A Survey of Elliptic Cohomology
            %p see there for background and context.
            %p
              This entry reviews basics of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/periodic+cohomology+theory"} periodic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+cohomology+theory"} multiplicative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theories
              and their relation to formal group laws.
          %p Next:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A+Survey+of+Elliptic+Cohomology+-+formal+groups+and+cohomology"} A Survey of Elliptic Cohomology - formal groups and cohomology
          %h2#rough_notes_from_a_talk rough notes from a talk
          %blockquote
            %p the following are rough unpolished notes taken more or less verbatim from some seminar talk – needs attention
          %p
            A complex oriented
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            theory (meant is here and in all of the following a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod) cohomology
            %em
              good notion of
              = succeed "es," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thom+class"} Thom class
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+class"} Chern class
              for complex
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
          %blockquote
            %p
              (this “good notion” will boil down to certain extra assumptions such as multiplicativity and periodicity etc. What one needs is that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+ring"} cohomology ring
              assigned by the cohomology theory to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}P^\infty \simeq \mathcal{B}U(1)
              is a power series ring. The formal variable of that is then identified with the universal first Chern class as seen by that theory).
          %p
            ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+class"} Chern class
            lives in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+cohomology"} integral cohomology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H^*(-,\mathbb{Z})
          %p
            or in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi K
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^*(-)
            where for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            we would set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_1(V) := ([V]-1)\beta
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bott+generator"} Bott generator
          %p
            In the first case we have that under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  c_1(V\otimes W) = c_1(V) + c_1(W)
          %p whereas in the second case we get
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mi β
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  c_1(V \otimes W) = c_1(V)c_1(W)\beta^{-1} + c_1(V) + c_1(W)
                  \\,.
          %p
            In general we will get that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+class"} Chern class
            of a tensor product is given by a certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+series"} power series
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi pt
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^*(pt)
          %p
            not all
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+group+law"} formal group law
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Landweber+criterion"} Landweber criterion
            gives a condition under which there is a cohomology theory
          %p
            %strong definition
            of complex-orientation
          %p there is an
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  x \in \tilde E^2(\mathbb{C}P^\infty)
          %p such that under the map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\tilde E^2(\mathbb{C}P^\infty)
                  \\to
                  \\tilde E^2(\mathbb{C}P^1)
                  \\simeq
                  \\tilde E^2(S^1)
                  \\simeq E^0({*})
          %p induced by
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}P^1 \to \mathbb{C}P^\infty
          %p
            we have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ↦
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \mapsto 1
          %p
            %strong remark
            this also gives
            = succeed "es" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thom+class"} Thom class
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn ∞
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}P^\infty \to (\mathbb{C}P^\infty)^\gamma
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn ∞
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∋
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\tilde E^2((\mathbb{C}P^\infty)^\gamma)
                  \\simeq
                  \\tilde E^2((\mathbb{C}P^\infty))
                  \\ni X
          %p
            Thom iso
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde H^{*+2}(X^\gamma) \simeq H^*(X)
          %p …
          %p
            (here and everywhere the tilde sign is for
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+cohomology"} reduced cohomology
          %p
            %strong definition (Bott element and even periodic cohomology theory)
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                An
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/even+cohomology+theory"} even cohomology theory
                is one whose odd cohomology rings vanish:
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi E
                          %mrow
                            %mn 2
                            %mi k
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^{2k+1}({*}) = 0
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/periodic+cohomology+theory"} periodic cohomology theory
                is one with a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bott+element"} Bott element
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi β
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi E
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta \in E^2({*})
                which is invertible (under multiplication in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+ring"} cohomology ring
                of the point)
              %p
                so that gives an isomorphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi β
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi E
                        %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %msup
                        %mi E
                        %mrow
                          %mo *
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                          %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)\cdot \beta : E^*({*}) \simeq E^{*+2}({*})
          %p
            Periodic cohomology theories are complex-orientable.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^*(\mathbb{C}P^\infty)
            can be calculated using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Atiyah-Hirzebruch+spectral+sequence"} Atiyah-Hirzebruch spectral sequence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H^p(X, E^q({*})) \Rightarrow E^{p+q}(X)
          %p
            notice that since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}P^\infty
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalent
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}U(1)
            (which is a topological group) it has a product on it
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{C}P^\infty \times  \mathbb{C}P^\infty
                  \\to
                  \\mathbb{C}P^\infty
          %p
            which is the one that induces the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}P^\infty
          %p on (at least on even periodic cohomology theories) this induces a map of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ℂ
                          %msup
                            %mi P
                            %mn ∞
                          %mo ×
                          %mi ℂ
                          %msup
                            %mi P
                            %mn ∞
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi ℂ
                          %msup
                            %mi P
                            %mn ∞
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi E
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo *
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi x
                          %mo ,
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mtd
                          %mo ←
                        %mtd
                          %mi E
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo *
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo ,
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo ←
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mtd
                          %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\mathbb{C}P^\infty \times  \mathbb{C}P^\infty
                  \&amp;\to&amp;
                  \\mathbb{C}P^\infty
                  \\\
                  E({*})[[x,y]]  &amp;\leftarrow&amp; E(*)[[t]]
                  \\\
                  f(x,y) &amp;\leftarrow |&amp; t
                  }
          %p
            this
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is called a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+group+law"} formal group law
            if the following conditions are satisfied
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %strong commutativity
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x,y) = f(y,x)
            %li
              %p
                %strong identity
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x,0) = x
            %li
              %p
                %strong associtivity
                f(x,f(y,z)) = f(f(x,y),z)
          %p
            %strong remark
            the second condition implies that the constant term in the power series
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is 0, so therefore all these power series are automatically invertible and hence there is no further need to state the existence of inverses in the formal group. So these
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            always start as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mi y
                  %mo +
                  %mi ⋯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f(x,y) = x + y + \cdots
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lazard+ring"} Lazard ring
            is the “universal formal group law”. it can be presented as by generators
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_{i j}
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi j
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i,j \in \mathbb{N}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi relations
                  %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %mn 3
                  %mi below
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  L = \mathbb{Z}[a_{i j}] / (relations 1-3 below)
          %p and relatins as follows
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mi j
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mrow
                          %mi j
                          %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_{i j} = a_{j i}
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 10
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 01
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_{10} = a_{01} = 1
                ;
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi i
                      %mo ≠
                      %mn 0
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall i \neq 0: a_{i 0} = 0
            %li
              %p the obvious associativity relation
          %p
            the universal formal group law we get from this is the power series in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y
            with coefficients in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lazard+ring"} Lazard ring
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℓ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi j
                  %msup
                    %mi y
                    %mi j
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\ell(x,y) = \sum_{i,j} a_{i j} x^j y^j \in L[[x,y]]
                  \\,.
          %p
            %strong remark
            the formal group law is not canonically associated to the cohomology theory, only up to a choice of rescaling of the elements
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+group"} formal group
            is independent of this choice and well defined.
          %p
            For any ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            with formal group law
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi power
                  %mi series
                  %mi in
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mi with
                  %mi coefficients
                  %mi in
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(x,y) \in power series in x,y with coefficients in S
            there is a unique morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \to S
            that sends
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℓ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
          %p
            %strong remark
            Quillen’s theorem says that the Lazard ring is the ring of complex cobordisms
          %p
            %strong some universal cohomology theories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M U
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+cobordism+cohomology+theory"} complex cobordism cohomology theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            is in degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 n
            given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi Thom
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mi standard
                    %mi associated
                    %mi bundle
                    %mi to
                    %mi universal
                    %mi bundle
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi E
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  M U(2n) = Thom
                  \\left(
                  standard associated bundle to universal bundle
                  \\array{
                  E U(n) \\ \downarrow \\ B U(n)
                  }
                  \\right)
          %p
            periodic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+cobordism+cohomology+theory"} complex cobordism cohomology theory
            is given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mi P
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∨
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℤ
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                  %mi M
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  M P = \vee_{n \in \mathbb{Z}} \Sigma^{2 n} M U
          %p
            we get a canonical
            %span.newWikiWord
              orientation
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/oriented+cohomology+theory"} ?
            from
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn ∞
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≃
                  %mi M
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi M
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\omega : \mathbb{C}P^\infty \stackrel{\simeq}{\to}
                  M U(1)
                  \\;\;\;\;
                  M U(\mathbb{C}P^\infty)
          %p this is the universal even periodic cohomology theory with orientation
          %p
            %strong Theorem (Quillen)
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+ring"} cohomology ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M P(*)
            of periodic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+cobordism+cohomology+theory"} complex cobordism cohomology theory
            over the point together with its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+group+law"} formal group law
            is naturally isomorphic to the universal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lazard+ring"} Lazard ring
            with its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+group+law"} formal group law
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℓ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (L,\ell)
          %p
            how one might make a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+group+law"} formal group law
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (R,f(x,y))
            into a cohomology theory
          %p
            use the classifying map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M P({*}) \to R
            to build the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi M
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  E^n(X) := M P^n(X) \otimes_{M P({*})} R
          %p
            this construction could however break the left exactness condition. However,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            built this way will be left exact of the ring morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M P({*}) \to R
            is a flat morphism. This is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Landweber+exactness"} Landweber exactness
            condition (or maybe slightly stronger).
